  {"name":"Folk customs and holidays","start":22544,"cards":[["At Japan’s Tanabata festival, wishes written on narrow paper strips are traditionally hung on what?","Bamboo","The colorful messages become part of a festival inspired by the annual reunion of two legendary celestial lovers.","Japan National Tourism Organization","https://www.japan.travel/en/sg/story/tanabata-a-wish-upon-a-star/",null,true,"easy",["Willow branches","Bamboo","Cherry branches","Pine branches"]]]},
  {"name":"Curiosities from the kitchen","start":22545,"cards":[["Some traditional cookie leaveners release which pungent gas in the oven, along with carbon dioxide?","Ammonia","Ammonium bicarbonate breaks down during heating. It suits thin, dry baked goods where the gas can escape, rather than remaining trapped.","US Environmental Protection Agency · Bakery ammonia emissions","https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-08/documents/eiip_areasourcesnh3.pdf",null,true,"hard",["Chlorine","Hydrogen sulfide","Ammonia","Sulfur dioxide"]]]},
  {"name":"A Cabinet of Sound","start":22546,"cards":[["After twenty minutes of a sustained note, what follows in Yves Klein’s Monotone Symphony?","Twenty minutes of silence","The prolonged quiet forms a deliberate second part of the performance, rather than a break between movements.","Museum of Modern Art · Anthropometry: Princess Helena","https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80530",null,true,"medium",["Twenty minutes of drumbeats","Twenty minutes of spoken counting","Twenty minutes of rising scales","Twenty minutes of silence"]]]},
  {"name":"Food History","start":22547,"cards":[["Civil War soldiers often softened rock-hard crackers in which drink, then skimmed off insects that floated to the top?","Coffee","Hardtack kept well but could be difficult to chew and infested; soaking made it easier to eat.","National Park Service · Civil War hardtack","https://home.nps.gov/mana/learn/kidsyouth/hardtack-during-the-civil-war.htm",null,true,"easy",["Cider","Lemonade","Coffee","Buttermilk"]]]},
  {"name":"Little Luxuries and Everyday Inventions","start":22548,"cards":[["What unusual motion distinguished Dyson’s short-lived washing machine introduced in 2000?","Two drums turning in opposite directions","Its paired counter-rotating drums were an ambitious alternative to the usual single rotating drum.","Dyson","https://www.dyson.com/james-dyson",null,true,"medium",["Laundry floating in a stationary tank","The entire cabinet rocking sideways","Two drums turning in opposite directions","A drum traveling up and down rails"]]]},
  {"name":"The Artist’s Back Room","start":22549,"cards":[["The tiny pencil insult on the original 1893 Scream was long suspected to be vandalism. Who did museum researchers conclude wrote it?","Edvard Munch","The artist himself added the sentence suggesting the picture could only have been painted by a madman.","National Museum of Norway · Research on The Scream","https://www.mynewsdesk.com/uk/nasjonalmuseet/pressreleases/national-museum-of-norway-infrared-scans-reveal-author-of-hidden-graffiti-on-edvard-munchs-original-painting-of-the-scream-3075318",null,true,"medium",["Outraged visitor","Art thief","Edvard Munch","Museum guard"]]]},
  {"name":"Ocean Life","start":22550,"cards":[["The barreleye fish can look through what transparent part of its own body?","Head","Its eyes sit beneath a clear dome and can turn to keep prey in view.","MBARI · Barreleye fish","https://www.mbari.org/animal/barreleye-fish/",null,true,"medium",["Tail fin","Stomach wall","Gill cover","Head"]]]},
  {"name":"Objects with a Past","start":22551,"cards":[["An offer for an ivory substitute led John Wesley Hyatt toward an early plastic while he was trying to improve which objects?","Billiard balls","A cash offer for an ivory replacement helped motivate Hyatt’s experiments. His celluloid billiard balls belong to the early history of commercial plastics.","Smithsonian National Museum of American History · Hyatt celluloid billiard ball","https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_2947",null,true,"medium",["Dice","Piano keys","Chess pieces","Billiard balls"]]]},
  {"name":"A Cabinet of Sound","start":22552,"cards":[["What produced musical notes inside the glass tubes of Frédéric Kastner’s unusual 1870s keyboard instrument?","Flames","Kastner’s pyrophone turned burning gas into music. Composers even wrote pieces for this remarkable relative of the pipe organ.","Science Museum Group","https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co5867",null,true,"medium",["Flames","Steel reeds","Spinning brushes","Falling water"]]]},
  {"name":"Deep Time and Distant Skies","start":22553,"cards":[["Which ingredient for making water does the solar wind deliver to the Moon’s surface?","Hydrogen","The Sun supplies hydrogen, while oxygen is already bound in lunar rocks and dust. Interactions at the surface can produce hydrogen–oxygen combinations.","NASA","https://science.nasa.gov/moon/solar-wind/",null,true,"medium",["Oxygen","Hydrogen","Nitrogen","Carbon"]]]},
  {"name":"Objects with a Past","start":22554,"cards":[["Inside an old coin-operated mutoscope, the moving picture actually came from a rotating collection of what?","Picture cards","Turning a handle rapidly flipped the slightly different images past the viewer, like a large mechanical flipbook.","Science Museum Group · Mutoscope Without Stand","https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co18328/mutoscope-without-stand",null,true,"medium",["Wax cylinders","Painted wooden disks","Glass slides","Picture cards"]]]},
  {"name":"Art & Sculpture","start":22555,"cards":[["At Walter De Maria’s kilometer-long sculpture in Kassel, visitors can see only the top of a brass rod. Where is the rest?","Underground","The rod descends vertically into the earth, with its small circular end flush with the surface.","Dia Art Foundation · The Vertical Earth Kilometer","https://www.diaart.org/collection/collection/de-maria-walter-the-vertical-earth-kilometer-1977-1977-002/object-type/sculpture/page/5/on-view/on",null,true,"easy",["Stretched through a neighboring building","Suspended beneath a bridge","Coiled inside a pedestal","Underground"]]]},
  {"name":"Small Wonders of Matter and Machines","start":22556,"cards":[["For IBM’s 2013 stop-motion film, the animators physically repositioned which microscopic objects between frames?","Carbon monoxide molecules","A scanning tunneling microscope moved the molecules on a surface. The tiny arrangements became successive frames of A Boy and His Atom.","Nico Casavecchia · A Boy and His Atom","https://www.nicocasavecchia.com/a-boy-and-his-atom",null,true,"hard",["Silica beads","DNA strands","Gold nanoparticles","Carbon monoxide molecules"]]]},
  {"name":"Folk customs and holidays","start":22557,"cards":[["In a traditional Korean birth custom, what did a decorated straw rope across the gate tell outsiders?","Do not enter","The geumjul marked a protected household after a birth. The museum account describes a twenty-one-day period without outside visitors.","Korea.net / National Folk Museum of Korea","https://www.korea.net/NewsFocus/Culture/view?articleId=290427",null,true,"medium",["Join the naming feast","Leave a written blessing","Bring a gift of rice","Do not enter"]]]},
  {"name":"The Cabinet of Fungi","start":22558,"cards":[["Microscope experiments found that airborne mushroom spores can encourage which weather-related process?","Water droplets condensing","Droplets formed again on spores placed in humid air. The researchers proposed that this ability could help cloud droplets grow, though the experiment did not measure a forest’s total rainfall.","PLOS ONE · Hassett et al., 2015","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26509436/",null,true,"medium",["Water droplets condensing","Ozone splitting into oxygen","Fog droplets repelling one another","Ice melting at colder temperatures"]]]},
  {"name":"Animal Encounters","start":22559,"cards":[["Which fish creates intricate circular patterns in seabed sand as a courtship nest?","Pufferfish","Male Torquigener albomaculosus sculpt the circles using their fins.","Natural History Museum · Pufferfish","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/pufferfish-underwater-balloon-of-death.html?pubDate=20250427",null,true,"medium",["Ray","Pufferfish","Lionfish","Triggerfish"]]]},
  {"name":"Wheels That Raise Eyebrows","start":22560,"cards":[["How did a driver reverse the 1921 Leyat Helica when its front propeller could only pull it forward?","Push the car","The lightweight Helica lacked powered reverse, so moving backward required a push.","Louwman Museum · 1921 Leyat Helica","https://www.louwmanmuseum.nl/en/news/new-acquisition-leyat-helica-from-1921",null,true,"medium",["Start a rear electric motor","Swap the drive belt","Push the car","Reverse the propeller blades"]]]},
  {"name":"Computers With Strange Pasts","start":22561,"cards":[["Early players of Spacewar! could control their virtual spaceships using a joystick salvaged from what?","Jet fighter","The PDP-1 game acquired a surplus aircraft joystick soon after its creation.","Computer History Museum","https://www.computerhistory.org/pdp-1/spacewar/?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_x-social-details_comments-action_comment-text",null,true,"medium",["Electric wheelchair","Construction crane","Film-editing console","Jet fighter"]]]},
  {"name":"Objects with a Past","start":22562,"cards":[["Some Bhutanese postage stamps issued in 1973 could play music because they were made as miniature what?","Gramophone records","The tiny playable records carried recordings such as Bhutan’s anthem or accounts of its history. They were also usable postage.","Smithsonian National Postal Museum · Talking stamps","https://postalmuseum.si.edu/glossary-t",null,true,"medium",["Music-box cylinders","Gramophone records","Magnetic cassettes","Piano rolls"]]]},
  {"name":"Deep Time and Distant Skies","start":22563,"cards":[["The first clearly identified planets beyond our solar system orbited what surprising kind of host?","Dead star’s pulsar remnant","They circle the dense, rapidly rotating core left after a massive star exploded.","NASA","https://science.nasa.gov/universe/exoplanets/will-the-real-first-exoplanet-please-stand-up/",null,true,"hard",["Dead star’s pulsar remnant","Newly forming Sunlike star","Supermassive black hole","Pair of red giant stars"]]]},
  {"name":"The Cabinet of Fungi","start":22564,"cards":[["Some fungi form tiny balls that termites carefully tend in their nurseries. What do the balls impersonate?","Termite eggs","The fungal bodies mimic both the appearance and chemical cues of eggs. Producing a particular enzyme helps trick workers into caring for them.","Current Biology · Cuckoo fungus and termite eggs","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19110429/",null,true,"medium",["Stored food pellets","Droplets of the queen’s food","Nest-building pellets","Termite eggs"]]]},
  {"name":"Buildings with Extraordinary Lives","start":22565,"cards":[["In 1827, Brunel’s guests dined with crimson drapes and a military band in what surprising location?","Unfinished tunnel beneath the Thames","The banquet celebrated the removal of floodwater from the tunnel, with gas candelabra lighting the tables.","Brunel Museum · Thames Tunnel Banquet","https://thebrunelmuseum.com/thames-tunnel-banquet-otd-1827/",null,true,"medium",["Unfinished tunnel beneath the Thames","Inside a ship’s boiler","Within a lighthouse lantern","Atop a moving railway carriage"]]]},
  {"name":"Feathers and Flight","start":22566,"cards":[["If danger approaches its riverside nest, where can a young hoatzin make a surprisingly effective escape?","Into the water below","The chick can swim, including underwater, before returning toward the nesting area.","University of Michigan Animal Diversity Web — Hoatzin","https://animaldiversity.org/accounts/Opisthocomus_hoazin/",null,true,"medium",["Into the water below","Into dense ground vegetation","Into a hollow branch","Into a burrow in the bank"]]]},
  {"name":"Wheels That Raise Eyebrows","start":22567,"cards":[["Where did the 1920s Megola motorcycle put its five-cylinder engine?","Inside the front wheel","The radial engine occupied the wheel itself, creating one of motorcycling’s more unusual layouts.","American Motorcyclist Association","https://americanmotorcyclist.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/202402-AMA2024-100years.pdf",null,true,"medium",["Inside the front wheel","Inside a sidecar","Above the rear mudguard","Beneath the handlebars"]]]},
  {"name":"Disney","start":22568,"cards":[["Disneyland’s Frontierland displays a massive anniversary gift linked to Walt and Lillian Disney. What is it?","Petrified tree stump","Disney family lore says Walt joked that the five-ton purchase was an anniversary present; Lillian later presented it to the park.","Disney Parks Blog · Walt Disney’s Legacy at Disneyland","https://disneyparksblog.com/dlr/walt-disneys-legacy-at-disneyland-resort/",null,true,"medium",["Steamship anchor","Petrified tree stump","Meteorite","Bronze elephant"]]]},
  {"name":"A Passport of Peculiar Places","start":22569,"cards":[["Most permanent residents of Whittier, Alaska, live in what unusual former military structure?","Single high-rise building","The former military Begich Towers now contains homes for much of the community.","Travel Alaska · Whittier","https://www.travelalaska.com/destinations/cities-towns/whittier",null,true,"easy",["A converted aircraft hangar","A retired hospital ship","An underground barracks complex","Single high-rise building"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22570,"cards":[["A 2023 study challenged the usual explanation for the crystal-like bladder cells on quinoa and common ice plant. What major function did the experiments reveal?","Deterring small herbivores","The cells formed chemical and physical barriers against arthropod attackers. In the tested plants, their role in salt and drought tolerance was much smaller than had long been assumed.","Current Biology · Epidermal bladder cell defenses","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37852262/",null,true,"hard",["Deterring small herbivores","Concentrating sunlight onto the leaf","Collecting pollen from the air","Storing oxygen for flooded roots"]]]},
  {"name":"Words with Baggage","start":22571,"cards":[["The word “alligator” absorbed the Spanish article el along with a word meaning what?","Lizard","English turned the two-word expression el lagarto into a single animal name. Even the little word meaning “the” became part of it.","Merriam-Webster · alligator","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/alligator",null,true,"medium",["Crocodile","Salamander","Dragon","Lizard"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22572,"cards":[["Researchers regenerated a flowering Silene plant from a prehistoric squirrel cache in Siberian permafrost. What did they grow it from?","Fruit tissue","The team cultured placental tissue from immature fruits, rather than simply germinating an ancient seed. The regenerated plants flowered and produced viable seeds.","Yashina and colleagues · Permafrost plant regeneration","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22355102/",null,true,"hard",["Leaf fragments","Dormant roots","Fruit tissue","Pollen grains"]]]},
  {"name":"Science in Plain Sight","start":22573,"cards":[["An odd recording of “sine-wave speech” may initially sound like whistles. What can suddenly make the same recording intelligible?","Hearing the original clear sentence first","Prior knowledge can make words pop out of the unchanged degraded sound. The listener’s interpretation changes even though the recording does not.","Matt Davis, MRC Cognition and Brain Sciences Unit · Sine-wave speech","https://www.mrc-cbu.cam.ac.uk/personal/matt.davis/sine-wave-speech/",null,true,"medium",["Listening with the ears exchanged","Hearing the original clear sentence first","Playing it slightly faster each time","Removing every pause between the sounds"]]]},
  {"name":"Railways Off the Rails","start":22574,"cards":[["George Bennie’s suspended Railplane would move along its overhead route using what?","Propellers","The design placed passenger vehicles above ordinary railway tracks to separate them from slower freight traffic.","National Railway Museum · Eccentric Engineering","https://blog.railwaymuseum.org.uk/eccentric-engineering/",null,true,"medium",["Rocket motors","Propellers","Paddle wheels","Sails"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22575,"cards":[["Scientists recreated a cyanobacterial daily clock outside living cells using a mixture of what, supplied with ATP energy?","Purified proteins","Three Kai proteins sustained a roughly daily cycle of chemical modification in a test tube. The system did not require ongoing copying of genes or construction of new proteins.","Science · Reconstituted cyanobacterial clock","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15831759/",null,true,"hard",["Purified chlorophyll pigments","Purified proteins","Isolated cell membranes","Purified DNA fragments"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22576,"cards":[["Cholla cacti can hitchhike on passing animals and establish new plants elsewhere. What are these spiny travelers?","Detached stem segments","Loosely attached segments break away when their spines catch a passerby. After dropping into suitable soil, the segment can establish a new plant.","National Park Service · Cholla cactus","https://www.nps.gov/places/cholla-cactus-cholla-cactus-garden.htm",null,true,"medium",["Detached stem segments","Clusters of leaf buds","Dried flower heads","Spiny seedpods"]]]},
  {"name":"Science in Plain Sight","start":22577,"cards":[["An Oregon State experiment on materials for electronics unexpectedly produced which useful artistic material in 2009?","Vivid blue pigment","The accidental product became known as YInMn Blue, after yttrium, indium, and manganese. Its striking color turned an electronics experiment into a pigment discovery.","Oregon State University · YInMn Blue","https://chemistry.oregonstate.edu/chemistry-news-events/yinmn-blue",null,true,"medium",["Fast-setting sculpting resin","Flexible clear varnish","Heat-resistant white glue","Vivid blue pigment"]]]},
  {"name":"Animal Encounters","start":22578,"cards":[["In a Berlin laboratory study, rats learned both roles in which familiar children’s game?","Hide-and-seek","They searched for a person or chose hiding places themselves, with playful interaction as a reward.","Einstein Center for Neurosciences Berlin · Rats play hide and seek","https://www.ecn-berlin.de/news-reader/rats-play-hide-and-seek.html",null,true,"easy",["Follow the leader","Musical chairs","Hopscotch","Hide-and-seek"]]]},
  {"name":"The Unlikely Science Bench","start":22579,"cards":[["IIT researchers used which sushi ingredient as the separator inside their experimental edible rechargeable battery?","Nori seaweed","The seaweed layer kept the battery’s electrodes apart. Other components included food-derived compounds, beeswax and edible gold contacts.","Italian Institute of Technology · Battery made from food","https://opentalk.iit.it/en/a-rechargeable-battery-made-from-food/",null,true,"hard",["Dried bonito flakes","Pickled ginger slices","Nori seaweed","Rice paper"]]]},
  {"name":"The Artist’s Back Room","start":22580,"cards":[["Ed Ruscha tested 75 substances on paper for Stains. Which product provided the ironic final entry?","Stain remover","The collection includes everything from steak juice to tap water. Its ending brings a cleaning product into an artwork devoted to making marks.","National Gallery of Art","https://www.nga.gov/stories/articles/delicious-daring-and-deadly-materials-art",null,true,"medium",["Stain remover","Shoe polish","Furniture wax","Laundry dye"]]]},
  {"name":"Little Machines and Unruly Ideas","start":22581,"cards":[["In a 2023 materials demonstration, a tiny metal figure escaped a barred cage using which trick?","Melting into a liquid","Magnetic particles embedded in low-melting metal enabled controlled movement and heating. The demonstration let the material flow through the bars before it was reshaped outside.","Matter · Magnetoactive phase-transitional material","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2590238522006932",null,true,"easy",["Dissolving in water","Folding into a paper-thin sheet","Separating into rigid blocks","Melting into a liquid"]]]},
  {"name":"Deep Time and Distant Skies","start":22582,"cards":[["When astronomers compared Jupiter’s northern and southern X-ray auroras, what surprised them?","Two poles pulsed independently","One pole showed regular pulses while the other varied erratically, rather than the two matching each other.","NASA","https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/dynamic-duo-jupiters-independently-pulsating-x-ray-auroras/",null,true,"hard",["Two poles pulsed independently","Both remained completely constant","Both brightened only during eclipses","Two poles alternated perfectly"]]]},
  {"name":"Curiosities from the kitchen","start":22583,"cards":[["In food science, kokumi describes an enhancement of what quality, rather than a separate basic taste?","Richness and mouthfulness","Certain compounds can make other flavors seem fuller and longer-lasting. Researchers have investigated the effect in foods and sparkling wine.","American Chemical Society","https://www.acs.org/pressroom/presspacs/2024/december/what-brings-richness-to-sparkling-wines.html",null,true,"hard",["Bitterness and dryness","Sourness and astringency","Richness and mouthfulness","Cooling and numbness"]]]},
  {"name":"Words with Baggage","start":22584,"cards":[["The unstoppable-sounding word “juggernaut” comes from a sacred title meaning what?","Lord of the world","English borrowed a form of Jagannath, a title of Vishnu. The ordinary modern metaphor has a religious name in its ancestry.","Merriam-Webster · juggernaut","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/juggernaut",null,true,"hard",["Keeper of time","Breaker of mountains","Lord of the world","Rider of storms"]]]},
  {"name":"Folk customs and holidays","start":22585,"cards":[["How does the sender of a Danish gækkebrev Easter letter disguise their signature?","Dots instead of letters","The dots reveal the length of the sender’s name. Guessing the identity determines who owes whom an Easter egg.","VisitDenmark","https://www.visitdenmark.com/denmark/inspiration/seasons/easter",null,true,"medium",["Replacing letters with numbers","Using a childhood nickname","Dots instead of letters","Writing the name backward"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22586,"cards":[["The orchid Epipactis veratrifolia tricks female hoverflies into laying eggs on its flowers. What larval food does it falsely advertise?","Aphids","The orchid imitates aphid colors and alarm scents, attracting mothers seeking prey for their larvae. Investigating flies transfer pollen but leave eggs without the expected food supply.","BMC Evolutionary Biology · Aphid mimicry in an orchid","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24621377/",null,true,"hard",["Animal dung","Rotting fruit","Decaying wood","Aphids"]]]},
  {"name":"Deep Time and Distant Skies","start":22587,"cards":[["A mountain chain on Saturn’s moon Iapetus runs along which imaginary line, like a seam around a ball?","Equator","Iapetus has an enormous equatorial ridge. The mountain chain is one of this two-toned moon’s most distinctive features.","NASA · Iapetus","https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/iapetus/",null,true,"easy",["Equator","Prime meridian","Arctic Circle","Tropic of Capricorn"]]]},
  {"name":"Animal Encounters","start":22588,"cards":[["As a young flatfish develops, what moves across its head to the opposite side?","One eye","The symmetrical larva transforms into an adult with both eyes on its upward-facing side.","Communications Biology · Flatfish eye migration","https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-024-05951-x",null,true,"medium",["One gill opening","One eye","One nostril","Its mouth"]]]},
  {"name":"Curiosities from the kitchen","start":22589,"cards":[["University of Southern Denmark researchers turned which gelatinous seafood into crispy chips using an ethanol-based preparation?","Jellyfish","The experiment changed the animal’s soft gel structure into a crisp material. It explored texture rather than ordinary frying.","University of Southern Denmark · Crunchy and crispy jellyfish","https://portal.findresearcher.sdu.dk/en/publications/the-microscopic-structure-of-crunchy-and-crispy-jellyfish/",null,true,"medium",["Sea cucumbers","Squid","Oysters","Jellyfish"]]]},
  {"name":"Words with Baggage","start":22590,"cards":[["The Italian name of vermicelli describes little examples of which creatures?","Worms","Vermicelli is the plural of a diminutive based on the Italian word for worm. The name describes the pasta rather than an ingredient.","Merriam-Webster · vermicelli","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/vermicelli",null,true,"easy",["Eels","Snakes","Worms","Leeches"]]]},
  {"name":"Small Wonders of Matter and Machines","start":22591,"cards":[["A 2022 University of Maryland battery used a gel derived from which discarded seafood material?","Crab shells","Chitosan from crustacean shells formed a biodegradable gel electrolyte for an experimental zinc battery. The study explored a different fate for seafood waste.","University of Maryland · Crab-Shell Battery","https://today.umd.edu/umd-engineers-get-cracking-on-sustainability-with-crab-shell-based-battery",null,true,"medium",["Tuna scales","Oyster pearls","Crab shells","Squid beaks"]]]},
  {"name":"Curiosities from the kitchen","start":22592,"cards":[["The Mexican delicacy huitlacoche forms when corn kernels are transformed by what?","Fungus","Corn smut produces swollen galls on the ears. The growth that farmers may treat as crop damage is also prized as food.","University of Wisconsin Extension · Huitlacoche","https://hort.extension.wisc.edu/articles/huitlacoche/",null,true,"medium",["A gall-forming wasp","A bacterial infection","A parasitic vine","Fungus"]]]},
  {"name":"Animal Encounters","start":22593,"cards":[["What surprisingly mammal-like bathroom performance did researchers film in tiny cicadas?","Urine jets","The studied cicadas expelled a continuous liquid stream. Their watery diet creates a large fluid load, making this efficient despite their small size.","Georgia Tech — Cicadas’ Unique Urination","https://news.research.gatech.edu/feature/cicadas-unique-urination-unlocks-new-understanding-fluid-dynamics",null,true,"medium",["Communal dung middens","Urine-soaked territorial trails","Buried dung pellets","Urine jets"]]]},
  {"name":"Tinkerers and Their Machines","start":22594,"cards":[["The CIA’s robotic catfish Charlie and Charlene were developed to collect what without attracting attention?","Water samples","The fish-shaped underwater vehicles could retrieve samples for analysis of possible contaminants.","CIA · Natural Spies","https://www.cia.gov/stories/story/natural-spies-animals-in-espionage/",null,true,"medium",["Lost radio transmitters","Water samples","Small pieces of ships’ paint","Underwater sound recordings"]]]},
  {"name":"Lights at the Edge","start":22595,"cards":[["In a spectroscopy demonstration, a salt-containing flame can cast a dark shadow when illuminated by which light source?","Sodium lamp","Sodium vapor in the flame absorbs the characteristic light emitted by sodium in the lamp. A brightly glowing flame can therefore also remove light from that beam.","University of Minnesota · Sodium-vapor absorption","https://spademos.umn.edu/7B11.25",null,true,"hard",["Mercury lamp","Infrared heat lamp","Red LED lamp","Sodium lamp"]]]},
  {"name":"Science in Plain Sight","start":22596,"cards":[["The tiny moving lights known as “blue-sky sprites” arise from which cells moving through vessels in the eye?","White blood cells","These are part of the blue-field entoptic phenomenon. Their light transmission differs from that of surrounding red blood cells; they are distinct from ordinary floaters.","American Academy of Ophthalmology · Blue-field entoptic phenomenon","https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/moving-spots-in-blue-sky",null,true,"medium",["White blood cells","Retinal cone cells","Pigment cells","Red blood cells"]]]},
  {"name":"Music","start":22597,"cards":[["Which technique lets a Mongolian limbe player sustain the flute’s sound while taking fresh air through the nose?","Circular breathing","The technique lets the flute’s sound continue while the musician replenishes the air supply.","UNESCO · Mongolian limbe technique","https://ich.unesco.org/doc/src/Side_event-Mongolia.pdf",null,true,"medium",["Circular breathing","Overblowing","Flutter tonguing","Double tonguing"]]]},
  {"name":"Odd Instruments, Albums and Screens","start":22598,"cards":[["Which Star Wars companion got his voice from a mixture of bear, badger and lion recordings?","Chewbacca","Ben Burtt assembled bits of animal sound to convey the Wookiee’s different emotions.","StarWars.com · Behind-the-scenes Chewbacca facts","https://www.starwars.com/news/8-behind-the-scenes-facts-you-might-not-know-about-chewbacca",null,true,"easy",["Yoda","C-3PO","R2-D2","Chewbacca"]]]},
  {"name":"The Botanical Bizarre","start":22599,"cards":[["Some acacias make their protective ants more dependent on them by interfering with which ability of the ants?","Digesting ordinary sucrose","A protein in the plant’s nectar inhibits the ants’ sucrose-splitting enzyme. The ants then rely on the host’s nectar, in which that sugar has already been broken down.","Ecology Letters · Nectar-mediated partner dependence","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24188323/",null,true,"hard",["Growing new sensory hairs","Recognizing the colony’s queen","Producing an alarm pheromone","Digesting ordinary sucrose"]]]},
  {"name":"Words with Baggage","start":22600,"cards":[["A word about good manners began with something much more tangible: the French ancestor of “etiquette” literally meant what?","Ticket","The word’s history passed through labels and written court protocols before referring more generally to proper behavior.","Merriam-Webster · etiquette","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/etiquette",null,true,"medium",["Napkin","Ticket","Handshake","Doorway"]]]}
